title='When does decision-focused learning add value to residential battery control? Private costs and AC feeder consequences'
d=pair('tou','dfl');cal=pair('tou','calibrated3');saa=pair('tou','stochastic20');sd=pair('tou','stochastic20',reference='dfl')
abstract=f'''Decision-focused learning can change residential battery schedules without improving physical forecasts, but its value relative to simpler calibration and scenario control remains an engineering choice. We evaluate matched training objectives and practical alternatives using public Ausgrid household traces, simulated batteries and AC feeder models. On 108,641 paired household-days, decision-focused learning reduces operating cost by {money(-d['micro_mean_difference'])} Australian cents per day relative to continued squared-error training. Three trainable offsets capture {100*cal['micro_mean_difference']/d['micro_mean_difference']:.1f}% of this benefit; 20-scenario control achieves a further {money(-sd['micro_mean_difference'])}-cent reduction relative to decision-focused learning. The latter provides no uniform voltage advantage over scenario control. Its matched voltage-exposure reduction is appreciable in the high-source-voltage benchmark, much smaller at lower source settings, and absent in two alternative networks without screening-band excursions. Phase-resolved AC sensitivities explain how altered battery injections transmit the signal adjustment. Deployable load substitutions separate poor frozen-signal transport from successful matched retraining. Continuous state-of-charge replay retains a cost benefit after inventory adjustment. Frozen transfer to ten Irish PV households supports time-of-use savings, while flat-price savings are inconclusive. These results identify where a task-trained deterministic signal provides a modest additional benefit and where calibration or uncertainty representation should take priority. The evidence concerns quasi-static constructed feeders and modeled operating costs, rather than field voltage compliance or battery lifetime.'''
intro=(M/'introduction.txt').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
intro=intro.replace('Feeder consequences require a separate assessment.', 'Feeder consequences require a separate assessment. Low-voltage PV–battery strategy comparisons already evaluate voltage and loss impacts [L35]. Recent Electric Power Systems Research studies address distributed-battery phase balancing [L36], stochastic storage flexibility [L37] and open-source predictive flexibility control [L38]. Grid-aware smart-home management also explicitly represents conflicting private and network objectives [L39]. These approaches optimize or coordinate system services, whereas the present comparison isolates the consequences of a separable, private-cost signal learner.')
methods=(M/'methods_foundation.txt').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
methods=methods.replace('Training and scheduling times are measured in isolated, single-thread processes on the same fixed sample counts.', 'Training and scheduling times are measured in serial, single-thread method subprocesses on fixed sample counts. Unrelated revision experiments run concurrently, so measured wall time is not an idle-machine speed claim; process CPU time is also retained.')
methods=methods.replace('Both fit only training examples', 'Both use the original frozen supervised PV checkpoint and reference HGB load forecast. They fit only training examples')
methods=methods.replace('The residual-scenario controller minimizes', 'The residual-scenario controller uses the original frozen supervised PV checkpoint and reference HGB load forecast, rather than the continued-MSE checkpoint, and minimizes')
methods=methods.replace('Primary contrasts average paired daily differences across three seeds before calendar-block resampling. New fixed-model engineering comparisons use seed 11 and explicitly retain that narrower training-replication scope.', 'The original deployment-cell contrasts and external seed-mean contrasts average paired daily differences across three seeds before calendar-block resampling. The expanded comparison in the Abstract, Table 2 and Fig. 1, and new fixed-model engineering comparisons, use seed 11 and retain that narrower training-replication scope.')
methods=methods.replace('Dates are synchronized across methods.', 'Intervals are pointwise and are not simultaneous confidence bands or adjusted for multiple comparisons. Dates are synchronized across methods.')
methods=methods.replace('This tests whether placing the correction in a separately named PV output is necessary.', 'This tests whether placing the correction in a separately named PV output is necessary. A revision audit found negative nighttime outputs in the unrestricted signed candidate, especially after cost calibration. The primary direct-net comparison consequently projects net demand to nonnegative values in the same fixed nighttime slots used by the PV support rule; the 24-offset calibration is refitted with that projection in training, validation and deployment. The underlying supervised network is unchanged. The unrestricted signed results remain in the Supplement. This support correction followed a revision diagnostic and is not presented as an untouched confirmatory design.')
methods=methods.replace('Limited power-factor and impedance brackets test physical-assumption sensitivity.', 'Gross-demand power factors of 0.90, 0.95 and 1.00 bracket the modeled reactive demand; 0.95 is the reference [L42]. Line resistance and reactance are jointly scaled by 0.90 or 1.10 in separate sensitivity cases, with transformer parameters unchanged. These interpretable brackets test sensitivity rather than estimating the unknown physical network.')
methods += '''

2.7. Operational transfers, protection and mechanism

The continuous-state variant removes the daily terminal equality and credits a fixed inventory value V(e)=λe−ρ(e−5)², with λ=(lowest purchase price+κ)/0.95 and ρ=0.01 AUD/kWh². Each controller still optimizes the next 24 hours. Test states carry across identical clean evaluation segments, initialized at 5 kWh at each segment boundary. This offline quality rule defines observable evaluation episodes; it is not an operational reset triggered by future measurement values. Newly matched learning uses two consecutive daily controllers, passing the first end state into the second, on 4,088 training and 1,021 validation anchors. It is a two-day differentiable training rollout, not 48-hour perfect foresight or full-year backpropagation. The realized settlement-plus-wear total is reported both before and after the boundary correction V(e_start)−V(e_end). The fixed terminal value approximates remaining inventory and is not an estimated future revenue.

Wear prices of 0, 0.01 and 0.04 AUD per throughput kWh bracket the reference. Throughput-based degradation costing has precedent [L43], but these values are declared accounting sensitivities rather than cell-calibrated aging estimates. For each value we distinguish repricing unchanged actions, reoptimizing with frozen signals and reoptimizing after matched retraining. This prevents an accounting decomposition from being interpreted as a new operational policy.

A separate prospective protection scenario imposes a 3 kW per-site export limit. Ausgrid's current connection guidance describes fixed-export options associated with changes from late 2026 [L41]; we do not apply that policy retrospectively as a compliance standard for 2012–2013. Given a previously planned battery action, PV is curtailed only as needed to meet the site limit, and the lost export income is included in actual retail settlement. All controllers receive the same rule. This tests a common post-scheduling protection layer, rather than matched retraining of every learner with an export-constrained optimizer. All three fixed panels are retained because the rule may bind very differently across capacity distributions.

For mechanism, we prescribe the first jointly clean date in each season at 02:00, 12:00, 18:00 and 22:00, plus the nearest exogenous gross-load-minus-PV samples to its fifth, fiftieth and ninety-fifth percentiles. At these 19 operating points, centered 0.10 kW/kvar perturbations estimate the AC Jacobians Sᴾ and Sᑫ of continuous nodal voltage with respect to demand-positive active and reactive injections. Steps of 0.05 check sensitivity stability. Actual load and PV are unchanged between methods, so ΔP=Δc−Δd and ΔQ=0 under unity-power-factor battery operation. Full AC replay evaluates Δv against SᴾΔP+SᑫΔQ. A complete daily signal/action/phase-voltage example uses the first date in this prescription, rather than the largest favorable effect. Neither this local approximation nor the binary screening metric establishes dynamic response or real-feeder causation.

2.8. Frozen transfer to a separate public dataset

StoreNet provides direct consumption and generation power records for an Irish energy community [L40]. We inspect all 20 household files and retain ten households with verified PV production. The downloaded power pipeline supplies minute-grid records; its missing values remain missing. We aggregate only complete half-hours and retain target days with complete preceding seven-day histories. The result is 2,224 paired household-days in 2020. The interpolated cumulative-energy files are not used. We simulate the study battery from gross consumption and production; Irish battery telemetry and a controlled-load circuit are not reconstructed.

Documentation gives differing nominal PV ranges. Therefore, a 2.2 kWp normalization reference is fixed before external outcomes, and 2.0 and 2.4 kWp are fully reported sensitivities, not verified household capacities or outcome-selected scales. Original Australian prices and source-clock calendar inputs are retained without hemisphere adjustment. No external target data fit weights, offsets or hyperparameters. The primary time-of-use checkpoints and analysis files are hashed before evaluation and verified unchanged. Six original flat-price checkpoints were inadvertently omitted from that initial manifest; their hashes and original timestamps are documented in an explicitly post-run addendum. We therefore give the flat group secondary transfer status, without retrospectively claiming a complete prospective freeze. External cost transfer does not validate Ausgrid household-to-feeder assignments or represent actual Irish bills.
'''
