stats=p0['outcomes'];scale=read('network_system_scale.json')['panels'][0]
results=f'''3. Results

3.1. The incremental benefit is small relative to practical alternatives

Expanded time-of-use comparisons include 108,641 paired GC/PV household-days from all 299 eligible households; 107,713 of these also support full-cost settlement including CL. DFL reduces paired operating cost by {money(-d['micro_mean_difference'])} cents per household-day relative to continued MSE (95% calendar-block interval for DFL minus MSE, {interval(d['micro_calendar_block95'])} cents; Fig. 1a). Household-average weighting gives a nearly identical {money(-d['macro_mean_difference'])}-cent reduction. Three-offset calibration recovers {100*cal['micro_mean_difference']/d['micro_mean_difference']:.1f}% of the mean gain, leaving an additional {money(cal['micro_mean_difference']-d['micro_mean_difference'])} cents for DFL. The 12-offset seasonal extension supplies no clear time-of-use gain over three offsets. Direct net-load prediction and its 24-offset calibration have higher realized costs in this implementation; this unsuccessful baseline does not establish that a separate PV representation is necessary.

Scenario control yields a {money(-saa['micro_mean_difference'])}-cent reduction relative to continued MSE and a further {money(-sd['micro_mean_difference'])} cents relative to DFL ({interval(sd['micro_calendar_block95'])} cents for scenario minus DFL). It also beats DFL in all four fixed feeder panels. Scenario control is therefore a substantive practical comparator. In the first feeder panel, DFL closes approximately 1.7% of the continued-MSE to perfect-information cost gap under a common seed and mask. That fraction measures scale, not the share of an otherwise attainable improvement. The three-seed, nine main deployment-cell results remain consistent with a modest DFL increment: approximately 0.787–1.236 cents per battery-owner day. Fixed-seed engineering extensions are kept separate from those training-replicated estimates.

Private gains are not universal (Fig. 1b). Under time-of-use prices, 96.7% of households have lower mean DFL cost, compared with 92.6% for calibration and 91.3% for scenario control. The largest household mean increases are 13.42, 2.41 and 19.24 cents, respectively, over their observed valid days. A favorable population mean thus coexists with a material adverse tail. These empirical household summaries do not estimate adoption welfare or guarantee benefits to a new customer.

The time-of-use DFL gain separates into 0.495 cents of retail settlement and 0.636 cents of throughput-proxy cost (Fig. 1c); the latter supplies 56.3% of the total. Calibration's retail term increases slightly while its throughput term falls. Flat-price results have a different mechanism: DFL reduces retail settlement by 2.322 cents while increasing the wear term by 1.105 cents, giving a net 1.216-cent saving. Scenario control lowers flat-price operating cost by 4.725 cents relative to continued MSE. Reduced throughput is consequently a condition-dependent accounting outcome, rather than a universal battery-life result.

3.2. Computational overhead is measurable but not a scheduling bottleneck

Incremental matched DFL training takes 385.6 s, compared with 21.8 s for continued MSE and 87.3 s for three-offset calibration. The scenario bank requires no iterative task-specific learning. Deterministic schedules take about 1.5 ms per household, versus 9.3 ms for 20 scenarios; all are small on a daily control timescale (Table 3). These timings include validation selection where appropriate and exclude shared supervised pretraining. Peak process memory includes loaded data and libraries. Concurrent unrelated work prevents interpreting wall-time multiples as hardware-independent speed ratios. DFL's maintenance distinction is the need to preserve and test controller gradients and refit a coupled task signal after material input or objective changes. This is a concrete engineering cost, but the experiment does not show that scenario scheduling is computationally impractical.

3.3. Voltage frequency, magnitude and thermal loading support different conclusions

At a 1.050 pu source on the first 55-household panel, DFL reduces overvoltage exposure from 18.751% to 17.901% and undervoltage from 0.02577% to 0.02318%, relative to continued MSE. The combined change is {stats['daily_voltage_pct']['delta']['mean']:.3f} percentage points (Fig. 2c). Zero-inclusive excursion falls from 0.0005914 to 0.0005671 pu. However, conditional overvoltage magnitude increases slightly, from 0.0031444 to 0.0031594 pu. Fewer observed events are therefore not equivalent to milder remaining events (Fig. 2d). The 99th-percentile voltage moves from 1.058341 to 1.058259 pu and the maximum from 1.071964 to 1.071583 pu. The largest exposure location and the largest instantaneous voltage need not be the same node; their individual summaries and continuous episode lengths are retained in Supplementary Table S4.

Source settings materially change the problem. At 1.025 pu, neither method exceeds 1.05 pu and the combined reduction is only 0.00848 percentage points, entirely from reduced undervoltage. At 1.000 pu, undervoltage falls from 1.4598% to 1.3588%, while its conditional magnitude rises from 0.0133455 to 0.0134594 pu. Wide-band samples below 0.90 pu remain at 0.04393% and 0.04151%, respectively; they have not disappeared. At 1.025 pu that wide-band underexposure is 0.001038% for both methods. The two SimBench cases have no 0.95–1.05 pu events for either method, making conditional excursion undefined rather than zero.

The complete threshold curves expose this dependence (Fig. 2a,b). At the reference source, 9.64% of continued-MSE samples lie within ±0.001 pu of the upper screening limit. Small continuous shifts can consequently move many binary classifications. DFL's modeled exposure reduction is informative for that benchmark, but it does not prove a substantial compliance improvement on an unknown field feeder. Power-factor and impedance brackets retain a combined reduction of approximately 0.734–1.083 percentage points; ten fixed mappings retain 0.667–0.965 points. Those checks establish stability within this constructed high-source case, while the lower-source and alternative-topology results delimit its usefulness.

The reference mean daily import peak is {stats['daily_import_peak_kw']['baseline_mean']:.2f} kW, reduced by {abs(stats['daily_import_peak_kw']['delta']['mean']):.2f} kW ({abs(stats['daily_import_peak_kw']['relative_delta_pct']):.2f}%). Losses fall by {abs(stats['daily_loss_kwh']['delta']['mean']):.3f} kWh/day ({abs(stats['daily_loss_kwh']['relative_delta_pct']):.2f}%). Export peak increases by {stats['daily_export_peak_kw']['delta']['mean']:.3f} kW ({stats['daily_export_peak_kw']['relative_delta_pct']:.2f}%). These movements cannot be summarized as uniform grid improvement. The unchanged 800 kVA transformer reaches 25.29% aggregate loading, with a maximum phase loading of 36.44%; the primary case is not transformer thermal congestion (Table 1). Conversely, the constructed 160 kVA rural SimBench case reaches 119.83% loading and exceeds nominal loading in 0.761% of valid half-hours, despite no narrow-band voltage events. Voltage safety and thermal loading are separate criteria.

Scenario control has lower costs, import peaks and losses than DFL in every fixed panel. Its overvoltage ranking changes across panels: DFL minus scenario exposure is about −0.343, +1.138, +0.132 and −0.207 percentage points for the three fixed panels and the held-out panel. There is no stable voltage premium that offsets DFL's higher mean private cost. Calibration and direct-net alternatives also produce different cost and network rankings. A network requirement therefore needs to be evaluated explicitly, rather than inferred from private objective alignment.

3.4. The mechanism is a timing adjustment transmitted through phase-coupled injections

On the first prescribed date, the task signal changes during daylight, yet the resulting battery differences also appear at off-peak boundaries (Fig. 3a,b). Because true gross demand and PV are held fixed, the injection difference is exactly the difference in discharge minus charge. The phase-resolved voltage response changes sign over the day and differs substantially between phases (Fig. 3c). On that date at 22:00, the aggregate demand difference is −2.417 kW and the mean voltage change is +0.000734 pu, with a maximum absolute receiving-node change of 0.002064 pu. The dominant sender and receiving location need not coincide; cross-phase effects also appear in the local contribution matrices.

Across the 19 prescribed operating points, the largest absolute discrepancy between local linear voltage prediction and full AC change is 5.01×10⁻⁶ pu (Fig. 3d). Halving the perturbation changes the predicted voltage difference by at most 6.62×10⁻⁸ pu. Eighteen cases have relative L2 discrepancy at most 0.216%; the summer 02:00 case has essentially zero action change and a voltage difference near 10⁻⁹ pu, so a relative error near one is a near-zero denominator effect and is retained. This explains modeled transmission of small action changes, without differentiating an exceedance indicator or validating unobserved network parameters.

The earlier shared-signal decomposition assigns approximately 90% of the first-panel private gain to its common component. That counterfactual uses test-derived corrections and remains diagnostic. The deployable three- and twelve-offset tests are the direct competing explanation: a simple trained correction recovers a substantial fraction, but not all, of the DFL gain. The mechanism supports a modest timing correction; it does not establish superior extraction of physical PV information.

3.5. Controller conditions distinguish transport from adaptation

Figure 4 separates changes to a frozen signal from matched learning in the changed environment. Replacing the reference load input with previous-day persistence changes frozen DFL minus MSE cost to +0.312 cents per day, with an interval spanning zero. Matching both learners to persistence restores a 1.475-cent DFL saving. The stronger deployable load mixture gives a modest validation RMSE improvement, from 0.5473 to 0.5423 kW; a single neural load model is inferior and remains reported. Neither that small error improvement nor the privileged true-load diagnostic should be treated as evidence that the task signal is a transferable physical PV forecast.

Under continuous SOC, matched DFL retains a 1.313-cent daily benefit. Raw operating and inventory-adjusted contrasts are nearly identical, and average daily terminal energy is about 5.22 kWh for both methods. The difference is consequently not explained by exhausting the terminal stock. The precise frozen-controller transfer and wear sensitivities are reported in Fig. 4 and Supplementary Table S6. Separate action repricing and policy reoptimization are retained even when they happen to agree. At zero wear, repricing and frozen-signal reoptimization both give a 0.495-cent benefit, while matched retraining gives 0.792 cents. Thus the gain is not wholly created by throughput pricing, although its reference magnitude depends substantially on that proxy.

A common 3 kW export protection layer retains DFL's matched private and voltage-frequency benefit on the three fixed panels. Its physical importance is heterogeneous: continued-MSE curtailment is approximately 0.0006, 10.676 and 0.090 kWh per feeder-day across those panels. The second panel loses about 1.553 cents of export income per household-day, compared with 1.584 cents under DFL. After repricing this protection, DFL minus continued-MSE costs are −1.123, −1.267 and −1.125 cents per day, and overvoltage changes are −0.850, −1.254 and −1.044 percentage points. This is evidence of survival under that common protection layer, not a substitute for voltage-responsive inverter dynamics or export-constrained retraining.

3.6. Separate data and cohort checks define portability

Frozen time-of-use transfer to the ten StoreNet PV households gives a three-seed mean DFL reduction of 1.252 cents per paired day at the fixed 2.2 kWp reference (95% DFL-minus-MSE interval, −1.741 to −0.767 cents). The 2.0 and 2.4 reference scales yield 1.573 and 0.803 cents, respectively. All scales remain reported. One seed's interval crosses zero; the evidence does not establish uniformly positive transfer across fitted models. Flat-price mean cost instead increases by about 0.122 cents at 2.2 kWp, with an interval of −0.194 to +0.458 cents. Secondary flat transfer is inconclusive, rather than a confirmed saving. These outcomes concern original Australian prices applied to a separate dataset, not Irish tariffs or independently measured feeder voltages.

The size- and load/PV-matched postcode check uses 27 households in each cohort, the same 27 electrical positions and 160 common clean dates. The difference between grouped and dispersed DFL-minus-MSE cost gaps is −0.070 cents per day (interval −0.286 to +0.118); the overvoltage-gap difference is +0.147 percentage points (−0.024 to +0.310). Thus the check supplies no clear directional advantage in either outcome. The grouped cohort has a 0.218 kW less favorable import-peak reduction. These are differences between constructed matched cohorts, not an identified geographic effect.

The private expansion avoids discarding usable households because another site is missing. The feeder masks remain selective: the first panel has no jointly clean dates in July 2012 or March 2013, and the second has only 129 of 365 test dates. Monthly counts and retained-demand/PV descriptions in Supplementary Table S1 identify that limitation explicitly. Temporal confidence intervals conditional on those masks do not create the omitted seasons or a new test year.
'''
discussion='''4. Discussion

4.1. An engineering selection rule from the combined evidence

The results change the question from whether a differentiable learner can obtain a statistically stable saving to whether that saving warrants a particular implementation. For an existing deterministic day-ahead controller, three trainable offsets provide an inexpensive structural correction with about half of the observed time-of-use DFL benefit. DFL adds roughly half a cent per household-day over that alternative. This increment may be relevant when the differentiable training infrastructure already exists and deterministic deployment is required, but the study does not estimate its break-even software or service cost. It is small enough that an engineering case should include maintenance, customer harm and operational requirements.

When 20 residual scenarios are acceptable, scenario control has the lower modeled mean cost under both tariffs. Its additional solve time is only several milliseconds, and the experiment reveals no daily scheduling bottleneck. The current evidence therefore favors considering uncertainty representation before deploying a task-trained deterministic signal solely for private cost savings. This conclusion is limited to the tested residual construction, price regimes and common battery. It is not a proof of universal scenario dominance, and it does not substitute for modern probabilistic forecasting evaluated under a wider set of objectives [L21,L22,L31,L32].

Network outcomes do not reverse that choice consistently. DFL reduces reference exposure relative to matched statistical training, but source voltage and topology determine whether a voltage problem is present and which direction dominates. The same DFL action may lower import losses yet increase export peak. Scenario control has lower import peaks and losses throughout the fixed-panel comparison, while voltage ordering changes sign. These results support evaluating network constraints or services directly when they are deployment requirements. Recent phase-balancing, stochastic-flexibility and grid-aware controllers already pursue such system objectives [L36,L37,L39]. The contribution here is to expose the bounded AC consequences of changing a private learner's objective under otherwise fixed information, rather than to replace coordinated management.

4.2. What the task signal represents

The learner modifies the optimizer's net-demand input. With a frozen demand forecaster, placing that correction in a nonnegative PV-shaped output is a modeling convention. The shift may compensate a combination of load error, PV error and the asymmetric operating objective. Cost gains cannot identify the source of the compensation. Matched retraining after persistence substitution establishes adaptation of a coupled learning-control system; the poor frozen transport establishes that its original output should not be passed to another input pipeline as if it were a calibrated conditional physical forecast.

Direct net-load prediction is conceptually consistent with that observation. Its weaker measured error and operating performance here prevents a strong empirical architecture comparison. Adding 24 cost-calibration parameters improves that candidate but does not remove its cost deficit, while its network ranking may be favorable. The evidence therefore leaves architecture necessity unresolved and does not use an unsuccessful baseline to justify a PV-specific high-dimensional correction. The low-dimensional calibration test is stronger evidence about practical complexity because it reuses the competitive common forecasting backbone.

4.3. Operational scope and unresolved uncertainty

Continuous-state replay and a sourced export limit address consequential boundaries of the reference controller. They show that the modeled incremental saving is not solely a daily-terminal or unconstrained-export artifact. The terminal value remains fixed, actions are open-loop within a day, and the export intervention is an identical protection layer after scheduling. Neither experiment reproduces a fully integrated inverter/controller with measured state error, degradation, network feedback and faults. Table 4 states supported conclusions and the stronger claims that would require different evidence.

The main transformer is lightly loaded and the upper screening limit lies near its retained source voltage. The broad voltage curves, near-threshold density and conditional severity show why a percentage-point reduction alone overstates practical importance. Conversely, the smaller rural transformer can be overloaded without screening-band voltage excursions. A field engineering assessment would require simultaneous voltage and thermal measurements and actual connectivity. Additional random assignments within the present data cannot supply those observations.

The independent StoreNet transfer strengthens one private-cost conclusion across datasets, while revealing a flat-price boundary and training-seed variation. Its normalization references are uncertain and its source calendar is deliberately unadapted. There is no claim that it validates future Australian weather, real Irish bills or the constructed feeder. Ausgrid evaluation remains informed by a test-triggered redesign, and the revision comparisons are recorded adaptations, not preregistered confirmation. Claims about economic returns, lifetime, transient protection or national adoption require observations not contained in these records. Concentrating those boundaries clarifies the useful conclusion: task training adds a modest conditional deterministic-control capability, while simpler calibration and scenario control remain competitive engineering choices.

5. Conclusion

Matched decision-focused training changes residential battery actions and their AC consequences, but the resulting private increment is small. A three-offset calibration captures a substantial share, and a feasible residual-scenario controller supplies lower mean private cost. Source voltage determines whether the observed voltage-frequency improvement addresses a present screening problem, and remaining-event severity need not improve. Deployable input changes distinguish frozen-signal transport from matched adaptation; continuous SOC and external time-of-use transfer retain a cost benefit under explicitly modeled boundaries. Engineering selection should therefore be based on the required information, controller boundary and private/network outcomes, rather than on forecast RMSE or the availability of a differentiable optimizer alone.

Data and code availability

Ausgrid source records are publicly distributed under their stated license [L30], and StoreNet records and documentation are available through their public collection [L40]. The European benchmark and SimBench networks are publicly described [L19,L23]. The accompanying reproduction package contains source-data acquisition instructions, cohort/date masks, model checkpoints, saved dispatch, derived figure data and executable table/figure and consistency checks. A public archival identifier has not yet been assigned. Local artifact availability is not represented as a public repository or DOI.

Declaration of generative AI use

Generative AI agents assisted literature discovery, research-design revision, Python implementation, computational analysis, figure construction and manuscript drafting. Assistance included code and quantitative analysis, beyond language editing. Source access limits are recorded, and key settlement calculations, masks, state transitions, optimization gradients and AC approximation claims were checked against saved outputs and independent computations. These checks do not replace human authorship review. No final human approval or authorship responsibility is asserted by an automated system; human authors must review the submitted work and complete the journal's declaration under their own responsibility.

Competing interests and funding

No competing-interest or funding status is inferred from the computational records. Human authors must supply the applicable declarations before submission.
'''
